{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019deTCMtCyaWN98Vd66C5Fo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODATA silicon lattice parameter 5.431020511 Å 22.5 °C\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787\"},{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425\"},{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2014\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1507.07956\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"doi.org\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1007/bf02026423\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2014\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2014.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Template:Physical constants\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst\"},{\"title\":\"on the need for a specified reference value for room temperature\",\"url\":\"https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/7D4C61179393A9A791B04BE22AB3442F/S0885715600014809a.pdf/on-the-need-for-a-specified-reference-value-for-room-temperature.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2010\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2010.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search confirms the value and its reference conditions, though the sources are mostly from the 2018 adjustment.\n\n- **Value:** A NIST X-ray table from the 2018 CODATA adjustment gives the lattice parameter of silicon at 22.5 °C in vacuum as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ (lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8).\n- **Later edition:** A Wikipedia template states that its silicon lattice parameter, a = 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, was updated to CODATA 2022 values (The values have been updated to the CODATA 2022 values.). The 2022 CODATA report itself says its lattice-spacing data were all already included in the 2018 adjustment (All data were already included in the 2018 adjustment.), which is consistent with the same number carrying over.\n- **Reference conditions:** The 22.5 °C figure comes from the ITS-90 scale, and the currently adopted reference conditions for natural silicon are t90 = 22.5 °C and p = 0 (vacuum).\n- **Context:** The lattice parameter is for an ideal, impurity-free crystal, and the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections. The {220} spacing is derived from it as a/√8, which is about 192 pm.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d8a163a-97ca-4d0f-b1af-a58e99a444fd","uuid":"364e8366-adeb-4212-a756-1b858fb4aae8","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:47:07.787Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CODATA silicon lattice parameter 5.431020511 Å 22.5 °C","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01J6KpfBqwG8gLRmkSbsNqm4","content":[{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787"},{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425"},{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2014","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1507.07956"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"doi.org","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/bf02026423"},{"title":"xray 2014","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2014.pdf"},{"title":"Template:Physical constants","url":"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst"},{"title":"on the need for a specified reference value for room temperature","url":"https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/7D4C61179393A9A791B04BE22AB3442F/S0885715600014809a.pdf/on-the-need-for-a-specified-reference-value-for-room-temperature.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2010","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2010.pdf"}]},"The search confirms the value and its reference conditions, though the sources are mostly from the 2018 adjustment.\n\n- **Value:** A NIST X-ray table from the 2018 CODATA adjustment gives the lattice parameter of silicon at 22.5 °C in vacuum as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ (lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8).\n- **Later edition:** A Wikipedia template states that its silicon lattice parameter, a = 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, was updated to CODATA 2022 values (The values have been updated to the CODATA 2022 values.). The 2022 CODATA report itself says its lattice-spacing data were all already included in the 2018 adjustment (All data were already included in the 2018 adjustment.), which is consistent with the same number carrying over.\n- **Reference conditions:** The 22.5 °C figure comes from the ITS-90 scale, and the currently adopted reference conditions for natural silicon are t90 = 22.5 °C and p = 0 (vacuum).\n- **Context:** The lattice parameter is for an ideal, impurity-free crystal, and the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections. The {220} spacing is derived from it as a/√8, which is about 192 pm."],"durationSeconds":5.16156584,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019deTCMtCyaWN98Vd66C5Fo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FtMUGPQcLyGfZ8U4yoqyeM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PBE lattice constant Si Ge diamond C Cu benchmark table Haas Tran Blaha 2009 zero-point corrected experimental\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050\"},{\"title\":\"Implementation of screened hybrid functionals based on the Yukawa potential within the LAPW basis set\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.4466\"},{\"title\":\"Ab initio study of lattice dynamics of group IV semiconductors using pseudohybrid functionals for extended Hubbard interactions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.07201\"},{\"title\":\"First-principles calculation of electronic density of states and Seebeck coefficient in transition-metal-doped Si-Ge alloys\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.10191\"},{\"title\":\"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3280356\"},{\"title\":\"www.arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1745\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529\"},{\"title\":\"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals\"},{\"title\":\"nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3331871\"}]\n\nThe search turned up a usable PBE table for C, Si, and Ge, but not the Haas/Tran/Blaha 2009 table itself, Cu values, or zero-point-corrected data.\n\n- **Semiconductor table (not Haas et al.):** A density-functional review tabulates PBE lattice constants of 3.573 Å for diamond C, 5.467 Å for Si, and 5.785 Å for Ge, against experimental values of 3.567, 5.430, and 5.652 Å. The table lists LSDA, PBE, PBEsol, TPSS, SCAN, TM, and experiment, with C (A4) at 3.573 for PBE and 3.567 experimental; Si (A4) at 5.467 and 5.430; Ge (A4) at 5.785 and 5.652. Its source is not stated as Haas et al., and it has no Cu entry.\n- **Other PBE/PBE0 data:** One LAPW study reports PBE lattice constants for C, Si, Cu, and others. The largest discrepancy in the lattice constant is found for Si, where a difference of 0.007–0.01 Å is found for PBE and PBE0. I did not retrieve its full numerical table.\n- **Haas, Tran, Blaha (2009):** The paper is confirmed as a journal article published in 2009 by Philipp Haas, Fabien Tran, Peter Blaha, and its DOI is 10.1103/physrevb.79.085104. Its general trend is noted in a summary: for bulk modulus, the ordering of functionals is reversed compared to lattice constant. The snippets did not include its benchmark table.\n- **Gaps:** I found no zero-point-corrected values in these results. For the Haas et al. table with Cu, check the PRB paper directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d8a163a-97ca-4d0f-b1af-a58e99a444fd","uuid":"c1549210-9a44-44d7-919b-4be6dfcbf92f","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:47:09.474Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"PBE lattice constant Si Ge diamond C Cu benchmark table Haas Tran Blaha 2009 zero-point corrected experimental","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GpdEzNe9EQrReGMTKFjC6k","content":[{"title":"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050"},{"title":"Implementation of screened hybrid functionals based on the Yukawa potential within the LAPW basis set","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.4466"},{"title":"Ab initio study of lattice dynamics of group IV semiconductors 